    },
    {
      "id": "u7gmbsSnWah1QjhuUIln_",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -448.1352560735137,
      "y": 2031.5638186476126,
      "width": 2005.36669921875,
      "height": 900,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0e",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 792310280,
      "version": 2397,
      "versionNonce": 27539720,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1761184299926,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The Outbox/Event Publisher is an entire workflow\nunder our Memory Service for Crucial Transactions...\n1) Each write request includes an Idempotency-Key header (unique per logical action).\n2) MemoryService calls Redis with `SETNX idem:mem:<key> \"in-progress\" EX 1800\n- If Success, the request \"wins\", otherwise it's a duplicate and is tossed with a 409 (if not done) \nor 200 (if done)\n3) MemoryService persists the new memory (or payment) inside a DB Transaction.\n- The same transaction also write an Outbox event row representing the downstream action that needs to occur.\n- Memory row includes idempotency_key for traceability.\n4) We mark completion in redis after a successful DB commit.\n- This would be: SET idem:mem:<key> {status:\"done\", memoryId:42} EX 1800\n5) Background worker periodically checks the Outbox table for unprocessed events.\n- Lock is: SETNX lock:publish:<outboxId> 1 EX 300\n- If lock is acquired, it publishes the event downstream (e.g. to kafka, redis stream, webhook)\n- If the lock exists, another worker has already handled it, so it safely skips.\n- The event row is then marked as published in Postgres.\n6) Now, if the app crashes, the DB still has the idempotency_key\n\nUsed for payments and memory events (to avoid double writes\nof the same memory)",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The Outbox/Event Publisher is an entire workflow\nunder our Memory Service for Crucial Transactions...\n1) Each write request includes an Idempotency-Key header (unique per logical action).\n2) MemoryService calls Redis with `SETNX idem:mem:<key> \"in-progress\" EX 1800\n- If Success, the request \"wins\", otherwise it's a duplicate and is tossed with a 409 (if not done) \nor 200 (if done)\n3) MemoryService persists the new memory (or payment) inside a DB Transaction.\n- The same transaction also write an Outbox event row representing the downstream action that needs to occur.\n- Memory row includes idempotency_key for traceability.\n4) We mark completion in redis after a successful DB commit.\n- This would be: SET idem:mem:<key> {status:\"done\", memoryId:42} EX 1800\n5) Background worker periodically checks the Outbox table for unprocessed events.\n- Lock is: SETNX lock:publish:<outboxId> 1 EX 300\n- If lock is acquired, it publishes the event downstream (e.g. to kafka, redis stream, webhook)\n- If the lock exists, another worker has already handled it, so it safely skips.\n- The event row is then marked as published in Postgres.\n6) Now, if the app crashes, the DB still has the idempotency_key\n\nUsed for payments and memory events (to avoid double writes\nof the same memory)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
